# SRG-APP-000363-AU-000180  -  CAT III
# The Central Log Server must be configured to perform on-demand searches of
# log records for events of interest based on the content of
# organization-defined audit fields within log records.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000363-AU-000180
group:           V-206496
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000158
control_rev5:    AU-7

contribution:    Searching is the product's core function; what the application configures
                 is that the general-purpose role which audit reviewers hold retains the
                 search capability and an index grant to search with.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

check:           authorize.conf [role_user] srchIndexesAllowed non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authorize.conf [role_user] search https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.3-configuration-file-reference/authorize.conf
retired_check:   authorize.conf [role_user] search equals enabled
                 reason: [capability::search] "Lets a user run a search using Search
                         Processing Language (SPL) or SPL version 2 (SPL2)." Granted only by
                         appearing in a role stanza with the value enabled.
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The organization's definition of the events of interest and the audit
                 fields that identify them, which the check text leaves
                 organization-defined
external:        Which roles the organization's audit reviewers hold, and any custom role
                 it defines in place of the shipped ones

citation:        Both conditions were written over [role_<roleName>], which makes them
                 undecidable rather than strict. Read over every role they fail the shipped
                 role_can_delete, which carries neither key (authorize.conf:240-248), and
                 capabilities and index grants are inherited through importRoles
                 (authorize.conf.spec:73-74, 79-84), so a role that holds search by
                 inheritance - role_power imports user - has no line to read and would be
                 failed for a capability it holds. Read instead as "at least one role" they
                 pass trivially on every install and are not evidence. Pinning both to the
                 shipped general-purpose role, where each key is always present in btool
                 output, gives a condition that can only fail where the capability or the
                 grant has actually been removed.
finding:         The general-purpose role no longer holds the search capability or has no
                 index grant, so on-demand searching of log records is not available to the
                 accounts that perform audit review.
